{
    "Insight Presentation & Visualization": {
        "analysis": "Sub-dimension 1 (Visual clarity & professionalism): The candidate report uses multiple explicit visualizations with descriptive captions (e.g., 'Health Grade Metrics Comparison', 'Health Score vs ROI Efficiency') and embeds them logically within the text. The baseline report includes only two charts (one bar/line combo and one scatter plot) with simpler labeling. The candidate’s charts appear more polished, with clear titles and takeaway statements that enhance readability, though we cannot see the actual images. The baseline’s visuals are functional but less described and likely simpler in design. The candidate report is more professionally structured, so I score this sub-dimension +2 in favor of the candidate.\n\nSub-dimension 2 (Chart appropriateness & accuracy): The candidate report includes multiple chart types (bar charts, scatter plots, distribution charts) tailored to different analytical tasks (comparison by grade, correlation between health and ROI, impact of priority and team size). Each chart is explicitly linked to the insight it supports. The baseline uses a combined bar/line chart for health vs. performance and a scatter plot for collaboration vs. ROI. Both reports choose sensible chart types for their data, but the candidate covers more dimensions and ensures visual evidence aligns with written conclusions (e.g., stating the scatter plot shows weak correlation). No misleading designs are evident in either. The candidate’s broader and more deliberate visual explanation earns a +1 here.\n\nSub-dimension 3 (Text–visual synergy & insightfulness): The candidate report excels in explicitly referencing each visual ('This scatter plot vividly illustrates...', 'The visualization confirms the insight...') and connecting charts directly to key drivers (priority, team size, complexity). Each chart has a 'Takeaway' section that highlights the insight rather than just restating numbers. The baseline report references its two charts but with less narrative integration; the scatter plot is described but not as tightly woven into the explanatory text. The candidate uses visuals to build a more compelling, multi-factor story, making insights easier to grasp. This earns a +2 in favor of the candidate.\n\nOverall, the candidate report demonstrates stronger visual presentation, better integration of charts into the narrative, and more comprehensive visual evidence across multiple dimensions. The baseline is adequate but simpler and less visually driven. Sum of sub-dimension scores: +5.",
