g., distribution of health scores, revenue tiers). The heatmap is appropriate, but the overall visual toolkit is limited.\n   - *Comparison*: The candidate uses a richer and more appropriate set of charts to illuminate different aspects of the analysis (distribution, composition, correlation), making the insights more accessible and accurate to interpret visually.\n\n3. **Text–Visual Synergy & Insightfulness (Candidate better: +2)**\n   - *Candidate*: Excels in this dimension. Each major chart is introduced, displayed (via reference), and followed by a dedicated \"Visual Takeaway\" paragraph. This practice explicitly connects the visual to the narrative, explaining *why* the chart matters and what key insight it reveals (e.g., \"This visual evidence directly validates the health score as a statistically meaningful leading indicator\"). The visuals are positioned as evidence, not decoration.\n   - *Baseline*: The text mentions the heatmap and refers to data in CSV files, but the connection is weaker. Insights are primarily described textually (e.g., \"Key Observations\") with the visual serving more as an appendix. There is less explicit guidance on how to read the chart or what specific pattern to extract from it.\n   - *Comparison*: The candidate's explicit, insightful commentary around each visualization creates a much stronger synergy between text and visuals, guiding the reader to the core message and enhancing the persuasive power of the analysis.\n\n**Overall Score Justification**: The candidate report is significantly stronger in visual presentation. It employs a wider variety of well-chosen charts, integrates them seamlessly with descriptive text and insightful takeaways, and presents the overall analysis in a more polished, professional format. The baseline report, while containing the necessary analytical components, is visually sparse and does not leverage visualization to communicate insights as effectively. The total score of +7 reflects a clear superiority across all three sub-dimensions.",
